<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  PROPOSAL 46 M3 : the last three pieces of per-track view state survive a
  save/load, and the arranger builds its rows from them.

  `fix/track-list-polish (m)` moved the FOLD set off the arranger and onto
  STrack, and `track_list_view_roundtrip.qxa` is its gate. THREE MORE were
  left behind in exactly the same shape : `STrack*`-keyed containers on the
  view, kept from dangling by a `pruneUiState()` walk called on every row
  rebuild:

    SStdMixerView::trackScale_    ->  STrack::laneHeightScale()
    SStdMixerView::takesExpanded_ ->  STrack::takesExpanded()
    SAutomationLaneUi::shown_     ->  STrack::shownAutomationLanes()

  So a user who made a lane taller, opened its take lanes and put a volume
  automation lane on it got all three back at their defaults on the next
  launch. The prune walk went with them: state that lives on the object dies
  with the object. Proposal 45 AC4.6 records that the walk did not reach
  SYSTEM lanes, so the master lane's copies of all three were being forgotten
  on EVERY row rebuild, which would have looked like three separate bugs.

  THE FIXTURE IS THE TAKE-STACK ONE because `takesExpanded` has to be gated
  twice over. `assert-lane-view` reads the TRACK, which since M3 is the only
  copy there is, so on its own it says the flag round-tripped through the file
  and nothing about what the arranger does with it. `assert-lane-alignment
  rows=` is the other half: expanded take lanes and a shown automation lane
  are extra ROWS, so a view that still consulted its own (now empty) sets
  would build the bare 2. legacy_takestack_wrap.qxp gives track 0 a two-take
  stack and track 1 as a plain sentinel, so the row arithmetic is
  2 base + 2 take + 1 automation = 5.

  THE ROW ASSERTIONS ARE DELIBERATELY ALL ON THE NEAR SIDE OF THE SAVE, and
  that is not tidiness : A SCRIPTED `load-project` DOES NOT REBIND THE
  ARRANGER. `SMainWindow::ensureArranger_()` returns the tab's existing editor
  whenever there is one, while `SLoadProjectAction` loads into the SAME
  SProject and gives it a NEW root mixer ("a load REPLACES the project, but it
  loads INTO this same SProject object"). So every arranger reach-through
  after a second load answers from the PREVIOUS load's mixer : measured here,
  a `set-lane-view laneScaleRow="0"` issued after a reload silently wrote to
  the old project's track and the model kept the pre-reload value. A row-count
  assertion placed after the reload would therefore be measuring the stale
  view and would pass whatever this branch did. That is a PRE-EXISTING testkit
  limitation, not something M3 introduced or fixes; it is recorded in the PR.
  `track_list_view_roundtrip.qxa` has the same blind spot in its post-load
  `secondWidth`/`scrollX` assertions.

  Watched failing on the pre-fix binary: with the three sets still on the
  view, every `assert-lane-view` after the `load-project` at the bottom reads
  the defaults (laneScale=1.0, takesExpanded=0, automation="").
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="lane_view_persists">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <load-project path="../legacy_takestack_wrap.qxp"/>

    <!-- The defaults, which are also what a project written before these
         attributes existed says: they are omitted entirely, so an older file
         re-serializes byte-identically. This baseline is the gate on the
         attribute-ABSENT path. -->
    <assert-lane-view trackPath="0" laneScale="1.0" takesExpanded="0"
                      automation=""/>
    <assert-lane-alignment rows="2"/>

    <!-- Move all three, through the REAL calls the mouse makes:
         setTrackHeightScale, toggleTrackTakesExpanded, toggleLane. -->
    <set-lane-view laneScaleRow="0" laneScale="2.5"/>
    <set-lane-view toggleTakesRow="0"/>
    <set-lane-view automationTrack="0" automationTarget="self:Volume"
                   showAutomation="1"/>

    <assert-lane-view trackPath="0" laneScale="2.5" takesExpanded="1"
                      automation="self:Volume"/>
    <!-- THE VIEW SIDE: 2 base + 2 take rows + 1 automation row. The arranger
         is live and correct here (nothing has been reloaded under it), so
         this really does say the sub-lanes were built from the flags. -->
    <assert-lane-alignment rows="5"/>

    <!-- Track 1 was never touched: these are PER-TRACK properties, not a
         project-wide one. -->
    <assert-lane-view trackPath="1" laneScale="1.0" takesExpanded="0"
                      automation=""/>

    <!-- None of the three is a one-way latch, and the rows follow them back
         down. Asserted HERE rather than after a round trip for the reason the
         header gives. -->
    <set-lane-view laneScaleRow="0" laneScale="1.0"/>
    <set-lane-view toggleTakesRow="0"/>
    <set-lane-view automationTrack="0" automationTarget="self:Volume"
                   showAutomation="0"/>
    <assert-lane-view trackPath="0" laneScale="1.0" takesExpanded="0"
                      automation=""/>
    <assert-lane-alignment rows="2"/>

    <!-- Set them again, and round-trip. -->
    <set-lane-view laneScaleRow="0" laneScale="2.5"/>
    <set-lane-view toggleTakesRow="0"/>
    <set-lane-view automationTrack="0" automationTarget="self:Volume"
                   showAutomation="1"/>
    <assert-lane-alignment rows="5"/>

    <!-- THE GATE. -->
    <save-project path="../../build/lane_view_persists.qxp"/>
    <load-project path="../../build/lane_view_persists.qxp"/>

    <assert-lane-view trackPath="0" laneScale="2.5" takesExpanded="1"
                      automation="self:Volume"/>
    <!-- ...and the untouched track came back untouched, so the attributes are
         genuinely per-track on the way out as well as on the way in. -->
    <assert-lane-view trackPath="1" laneScale="1.0" takesExpanded="0"
                      automation=""/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="2"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
